turning to a growing problem in the  world's oceans lost and abandoned  fishing gear what's known as ghost gear  litters the sea floor threatening marine  life and the livelihoods of fishermen  around the globe in maine for instance  an estimated get this 10 of all lobster  traps are lost every year which adds up  to about 280 000 traps in all for our  series i on earth nancy chen traveled to  portland maine to find out what's been  done to solve the problem  on the surface portland maine's  coastline looks like a classic new  england picture postcard  but lurking just a few feet below  another world  one of long forgotten fishing nets ropes  and lobster traps tangled together over  years by storms and currents  if you throw it over the side it never  leaves it's still there  lobsterman jim buxton has worked the  waters off portland for more than 30  years  he led us three miles off shore to  whitehead passage where we watched him  haul up today's catch a ball of lost and  abandoned fishing gear twisted with his  own that's probably six or eight traps  than the  rope for a string and  a piece of gill net that was drifting  around the bottom how did you find it it  was wrapped up in some lobster gear i  was actually actively fishing this is  gilnet  it's known as ghost gear that's a  trapdoor this is a shrimp twine head  which we started using in the late 80s  early 90s how long do you think this has  been under water  i'm estimating like 15 years plus in  2019 buxton helped recover this seven  ton ball of ghost gear off nearby cape  elizabeth but that's just a small  fraction of the more than 600 thousand  tons of fishing gear thought to be added  to the world's oceans each year it's  really something that is out of sight  out of mind for most folks jacqueline  mcgarry is the program manager for the  global ghost care initiative or triple  gi at the ocean conservancy the triple  gi is an alliance of 18 nations dozens  of companies universities and ngos ghost  gear is the most harmful form of marine  debris to wildlife  but it is also really harmful to fishers  and coastal communities who depend on  income from fishing and it is a problem  pretty much anywhere you find fishing  anywhere in the world  all over the world  ghost skier can unintentionally trap  injure or even kill marine life last  year divers freed this whale from a  tangled fishing net off the coast of  italy is the solution to all this in the  prevention or in the removal of ghost  gear it really is about both preventing  it from ending up in our ocean in the  first place  minimizing the amount of damage it does  and then recovering what's easily able  to be recovered  like the ghost gear buxton found on a  dive at the end of a fishing pier in  downtown portland  there was so much a crane had to be  brought in to lift it out of the water i  mean this is a challenge aaron paletier  runs the gulf of maine lobster  foundation and works with the triple gi  and fishermen like buxton to organize  local cleanups you know when you're  pulling it up out of the water and  you're waiting to see  what it is it's like waiting for  christmas  wow what is this  among the finds a door ladder and plenty  of traps nets and rope about two tons in  all  that hits you right in the nostrils  holy moly no one had really approached  the subject before fishermen in general  don't want their fingers pointed at them  because this is nothing they do on  purpose but they started to realize why  don't we be stewards of the environment  and help with the process rather than  pretend it's not there how important is  it to have that partnership with them  it's critical and they know where the  stuff is  they're the ones who get on the water i  don't dive so  we wouldn't be able to do it without  them a sometimes tedious task in what  can feel like just a drop in the ocean  but buxton wouldn't have it any other  way how do you feel bringing this stuff  up  good i've been a diver since 83  the average person never sees the bar  this is part of fishing you know and i  i'm a fisherman i feel responsible to  help clean it up  removing the ghost gear of years past  and keeping these waters full of life  for cbs mornings i'm ninci chen in  portland maine  it's amazing what's down there and  that's a big project i wish them the  best but while they're down there i did  lose a pair of sunglasses in the  chesapeake in the 90s if they see them  yeah can i get them back part of the 600  000 tons of stuff that's not for sherman  gear there's a lot of stuff down there  it's what we don't see yeah cars wallets  treasure  lots of it what i love about sunglasses  what i love about these pieces is it  brings to light something most people  don't think about  and then the fact that they're actually  doing something about it because a lot  of times when you think of climate and  these ion earth segments you kind of  leave feeling like what's the solution  what yeah are we going to do it's the  places we will never get our eyes on so  for them to give us that eye-opening  story absolutely it's important for us  to know these things for sure for sure i  on earth
 

